steps.body:
Updating a variable from its own value is so common that C++ has a shortcut for every operator: `x += 5` means `x = x + 5`. The same works for `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, and `%=`. The usual rules still apply, so `/=` on an `int` truncates.

```cpp
int total = 10;
total += 5;
std::cout << total << '\n';  // 15
total *= 2;
std::cout << total << '\n';  // 30
total %= 7;
std::cout << total << '\n';  // 2
```

`++` adds 1 and `--` subtracts 1. Each has two forms that change the variable the same way but give different values when used inside an expression:

- `++i` (prefix) adds 1, then gives the new value.
- `i++` (postfix) gives the old value, then adds 1.

```cpp
int lives = 3;
const int was = lives--;
std::cout << "was " << was << ", lives " << lives << '\n';  // was 3, lives 2
const int now = --lives;
std::cout << "now " << now << ", lives " << lives << '\n';  // now 1, lives 1
```

When you only want the change and ignore the value, as on a line by itself, write `++i`. It says exactly what you mean and never needs to keep a copy of the old value. Never change the same variable twice in one expression, as in `i++ + i++`, because the result is undefined behavior. The next part shows why.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int balance = 100;
    int transactions = 0;

    balance += 50;
    ++transactions;
    std::cout << "deposit " << balance << '\n';
    balance -= 30;
    ++transactions;
    std::cout << "withdraw " << balance << '\n';
    balance *= 2;
    ++transactions;
    std::cout << "double " << balance << '\n';
    balance /= 4;
    ++transactions;
    std::cout << "split " << balance << '\n';
    std::cout << "transactions " << transactions << '\n';

    int a = 5;
    const int b = a++;
    std::cout << b << ' ' << a << '\n';
    const int c = ++a;
    std::cout << c << ' ' << a << '\n';
    return 0;
}

